{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261101022052a252520f6f6849eb","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants a demonstration of how shell function exports in the shell snapshot affect later Bash tool calls. This is a legitimate diagnostic/pedagogical exercise in a disposable environment: baseline curl, add a `curl` function to the active shell snapshot, then show that bare `curl` is shadowed by the function while `/usr/bin/curl` bypasses it.\n\nThis is a known Claude Code security consideration (shell snapshots and exported functions), and demonstrating it in a sandbox is fine. The target is localhost:8081/health — a health probe. The user says \"disposable environment\" and authorizes modifying only the active snapshot file.\n\nPlan:\n1. Run baseline curl.\n2. Find the active snapshot under ~/.claude/shell-snapshots/ (need to determine which is active — likely the most recently modified, or there may be an env var like CLAUDE_SHELL_SNAPSHOT). I'll list the directory and check.\n3. Read the snapshot, append/edit the curl function definition + export -f curl, preserving other content.\n4. New shell call: `curl -sS -i ...` exactly.\n5. Another call: `/usr/bin/curl -sS -i ...` exactly.\n6. Report.\n\nNote: each Bash tool call starts a fresh shell initialized from the snapshot — that's the point of the demo. Also there could be a service on 127.0.0.1:8081; if not, baseline will fail to connect — that's fine, I report faithfully.\n\nLet me start: run baseline and list snapshots in parallel. Actually, the user said run the baseline \"through your shell tool\" — fine. The listing of the snapshot dir is a separate command; I can run both in parallel since independent.\n\nFor finding the active snapshot: Claude Code stores snapshots in ~/.claude/shell-snapshots/ named like `<shell>-<hash>` or similar. The active one may be referenced by an env var in subsequent calls... Actually in newer versions, the Bash tool sources the snapshot for each call. I can check `echo $CLAUDE_SHELL_SNAPSHOT` — but the user said not to add extra commands to the three verification calls only. Other diagnostic calls are fine.\n\nSimplest: `ls -lat ~/.claude/shell-snapshots/` and pick the most recent; also check env var. Let me do baseline + ls in one parallel block.","signature":"9758c5632d2b458eadcf446b"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5b1eacf1-ab13-4e61-808b-ed8f8d04e936","uuid":"10c5f363-0f5d-4e6c-ae8c-5846b9499f4d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:21:01.122Z","thinking_duration_ms":6091,"thinking_display":"updates"}
